ggml: the f32-to-f16 conversion gives inf past the f16 range with fast math
Fold-into: upstream/ggml-core

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

ggml_compute_fp32_to_fp16 (ggml-impl.h) calculates base = (|f| * 2^112) *
2^-110. The first product is inf for |f| >= 65520, and thus the result is inf.
With reassociation (for example -ffp-model=fast, the flags of the app build),
clang changes the two products into one product by 4. Then the function gives
a finite value or a NaN for these values: 0x7c46 (a NaN) for 70000, 0x0ba1
for 1e6 and 0x53dc for 2.7e11. A test of each of the 2^32 f32 values against
the F16C conversion gives 1828646904 differences, and each one is a value that
must give inf.

On x86, the CPU backend uses this function for each scalar f16 value
(GGML_CPU_FP32_TO_FP16), for example in CPY to f16. The op fuzzer found the
defect in the x86 release build: CPY of f32[192] to f16 gave the finite value
0.0034 where the correct value is inf. On arm64, the CPU backend uses the
hardware conversion, but ggml-base (ggml_fp32_to_fp16_row), the reference
quantizers of ggml-quants.c and the Hexagon host code use this function. The
app graphs do not reach this condition: no value that they convert to f16 is
near 65504 (the largest is 205), and the Hexagon host code sets a value more
than 65504 to 65504 first.

This patch adds an integer compare of the bits of |f| with the bits of 65520
before the float calculation. For |f| >= 65520 that is not a NaN, the function
gives inf with the sign of f. The compare does not depend on the float flags,
and the result of each other value does not change. With the release flags,
the path costs one integer add, one compare and one branch on x86 and on
aarch64: the compiler makes one unsigned compare from the two limits. The test
of the 2^32 values gives 0 differences with the precise flags and with
-ffp-model=fast.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-impl.h b/ggml/src/ggml-impl.h
index ae26e0c..87b7095 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-impl.h
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-impl.h
@@ -429,6 +429,13 @@ static inline ggml_fp16_t ggml_compute_fp32_to_fp16(float f) {
     const uint32_t w = fp32_to_bits(f);
     const uint32_t shl1_w = w + w;
     const uint32_t sign = w & UINT32_C(0x80000000);
+    // The f16 value of |f| >= 65520 (0x477FF000) is inf. The two products of base give inf for such
+    // a value, but a compiler with reassociation (for example -ffp-model=fast) can change them into
+    // one product by 4. Then base gives a finite value or a NaN. This integer compare does not
+    // depend on the float flags, and it does not include a NaN (shl1_w > 0xFF000000).
+    if (shl1_w >= UINT32_C(0x8EFFE000) && shl1_w <= UINT32_C(0xFF000000)) {
+        return (sign >> 16) | UINT16_C(0x7C00);
+    }
     uint32_t bias = shl1_w & UINT32_C(0xFF000000);
     if (bias < UINT32_C(0x71000000)) {
         bias = UINT32_C(0x71000000);
